LVM Management
==========================
1. What is LVM in Linux ?
✔ Logical Volume Manager (LVM) is used for flexible disk management
✔ Allows dynamic resizing of partitions
✔ Abstracts physical storage into logical layers
👉 Layers:
Physical Disk → PV → VG → LV → Filesystem

2. What are LVM components ?
Step 1: Physical Volume (PV) → Raw disk (/dev/sdb)
Step 2: Volume Group (VG) → Pool of storage
Step 3: Logical Volume (LV) → Usable partition
Step 4: Filesystem → ext4/xfs mounted for use

3. Types of LVM
Type 1: Linear LVM
✔ Data stored sequentially
Type 2: Striped LVM
✔ Data spread across disks (performance ↑)
Type 3: Mirrored LVM
✔ Data duplicated (redundancy ↑)
Type 4: Thin Provisioning
✔ Allocate space on demand

4. How to create LVM (end-to-end) ?
Step 1: Create PV
pvcreate /dev/sdb
Step 2: Create VG
vgcreate myvg /dev/sdb
Step 3: Create LV
lvcreate -L 10G -n mylv myvg
Step 4: Format
mkfs.xfs /dev/myvg/mylv
Step 5: Mount
mount /dev/myvg/mylv /mnt

5. Important LVM commands
PV commands:
pvcreate /dev/sdb
pvdisplay
pvs
VG commands:
vgcreate myvg /dev/sdb
vgextend myvg /dev/sdc
vgdisplay
vgs
LV commands:
lvcreate -L 5G -n lv1 myvg
lvdisplay
lvs
lvremove /dev/myvg/lv1

6. How to reduce LVM size ?
⚠ Only for ext4 (not recommended in production)
Step 1: Unmount
umount /mnt
Step 2: Check filesystem
e2fsck -f /dev/myvg/mylv
Step 3: Reduce filesystem
resize2fs /dev/myvg/mylv 5G
Step 4: Reduce LV
lvreduce -L 5G /dev/myvg/mylv
Step 5: Mount again
mount /mnt

7. How to extend root filesystem (production case) ?
Step 1: Check disk
lsblk
Step 2: Create PV
pvcreate /dev/sdb
Step 3: Extend VG
vgextend rootvg /dev/sdb
Step 4: Extend LV
lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/rootvg/root
Step 5: Resize FS
xfs_growfs /

15. Interview scenario: Disk full on server
Step 1: Check usage
df -h
Step 2: Check LVM
lvs
Step 3: Add new disk
/dev/sdb
Step 4: Extend VG
vgextend myvg /dev/sdb
Step 5: Extend LV + FS
lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/myvg/mylv
xfs_growfs /mnt

1. What happens if one disk in VG fails ?
Step 1: If no redundancy → data loss
Step 2: VG becomes partial
Step 3: LV becomes inaccessible
Step 4: Use vgreduce --removemissing (risk data loss)
👉 Solution: Use RAID / Mirroring

2. Can you extend mounted filesystem ?
Step 1: Yes, for XFS/ext4
Step 2: Extend LV
lvextend -L +5G /dev/myvg/mylv
Step 3: Resize FS
xfs_growfs /mnt

3. Can you reduce mounted filesystem ?
Step 1: No (must be unmounted)
Step 2: Only ext4 supported
Step 3: High risk → backup required

4. What happens if snapshot space gets full ?
Step 1: Snapshot becomes invalid
Step 2: Data corruption risk
Step 3: Snapshot automatically dropped
👉 Always allocate enough space

5. Difference between lvextend and lvresize ?
lvextend → Increase only
lvresize → Increase + decrease

6. Why LVM is better than partition ?
Step 1: Dynamic resizing
Step 2: Add disks without downtime
Step 3: Snapshots
Step 4: Better storage management

7. How to recover deleted LV ?
Step 1: Check metadata backup
ls /etc/lvm/archive
Step 2: Restore
vgcfgrestore myvg
👉 Not always guaranteed

8. What is PE and LE ?
PE → Physical Extent (VG level)
LE → Logical Extent (LV level)
👉 Mapping: LE → PE

9. What is stripe vs mirror in LVM ?
Stripe → Performance ↑ (spread IO)
Mirror → Redundancy ↑ (duplicate data)

10. Can one PV belong to multiple VGs ?
❌ No
✔ One PV → One VG only

11. What happens if VG is inactive ?
Step 1: LV not accessible
Step 2: Mount fails
Fix:
vgchange -ay

12. Difference between thin and thick provisioning ?
Thin → Allocate on demand
Thick → Pre-allocated
👉 Risk: Thin can over-allocate

13. Why lvextend works but filesystem still same ?
Step 1: LV extended only
Step 2: FS not resized
Fix:
xfs_growfs /mountpoint

14. How to move data from one disk to another in VG ?
Step 1: Add new disk
pvcreate /dev/sdc
vgextend myvg /dev/sdc
Step 2: Move data
pvmove /dev/sdb /dev/sdc

15. What is pvmove used for ?
✔ Move data between disks
✔ Used in disk replacement

16. What happens if you remove PV without vgreduce ?
Step 1: VG breaks
Step 2: LV corrupted
Step 3: Data loss possible

17. How to check free space in VG ?
Command:
vgs
OR
vgdisplay

18. Can you mount LV without filesystem ?
❌ No
✔ Must format first
mkfs.xfs /dev/myvg/mylv

19. What is metadata in LVM ?
✔ Stores VG, LV, PV mapping
✔ Stored in:
/etc/lvm/archive
👉 Critical for recovery

20. Real scenario: Server crashed after disk removal
Step 1: Boot in rescue mode
Step 2: Activate VG
vgchange -ay
Step 3: Check missing PV
pvs
Step 4: Remove missing disk
vgreduce --removemissing
Step 5: Repair FS
fsck

1. What is /etc/fstab in Linux ?
✔ File used for automatic mounting at boot
✔ Defines filesystem, mount point, options

Structure of /etc/fstab
Step 1: Device
Step 2: Mount Point
Step 3: Filesystem Type
Step 4: Options
Step 5: Dump
Step 6: Pass

Example entry
/dev/sdb1    /data    xfs    defaults    0 0

Fields explanation
Step 1: Device
/dev/sdb1 OR UUID
Step 2: Mount point
/data
Step 3: Filesystem
xfs / ext4
Step 4: Options
defaults / noexec / nosuid
Step 5: Dump
0 → disable backup
Step 6: Pass
0 → no fsck
1 → root fs
2 → other fs

Important mount options
defaults → rw, suid, exec
ro → read only
noexec → disable execution
nosuid → no SUID
nodev → ignore device files
auto → mount at boot
noauto → manual mount only

Best practice (production)
Step 1: Use UUID instead of device
blkid
Step 2: Add entry
UUID=xxxx    /data    xfs    defaults    0 0

How to verify fstab without reboot
Step 1: Test config
mount -a
👉 If error → fstab issue

Common troubleshooting
Step 1: System stuck at boot
Reason: Wrong fstab entry
Step 2: Boot in rescue mode
Step 3: Edit file
vi /etc/fstab
Step 4: Comment problematic line


2. Restore LVM from backup (/etc/lvm/backup)
✔ LVM automatically stores metadata backup
✔ Location:
/etc/lvm/backup/
/etc/lvm/archive/


3. How LVM backup works ?
Step 1: Every change (vgcreate, lvextend)
Step 2: Metadata saved automatically
Command to manually backup:
vgcfgbackup

4. Restore Volume Group (VG) from backup
Step-by-step
Step 1: Check available backups
ls /etc/lvm/backup
OR
ls /etc/lvm/archive

Step 2: View backup file
cat /etc/lvm/backup/myvg

Step 3: Restore metadata
vgcfgrestore myvg

Step 4: Activate VG
vgchange -ay myvg


5. Full recovery scenario (disk issue / metadata corruption)
Case: VG not detected
Step 1: Scan for LVM
pvscan
vgscan

Step 2: Restore metadata
vgcfgrestore myvg

Step 3: Activate
vgchange -ay

Step 4: Verify
lvs
vgs
pvs


6. Restore after disk replacement
Step 1: Replace failed disk
Step 2: Create PV
pvcreate /dev/sdb

Step 3: Restore metadata
vgcfgrestore myvg

Step 4: Rebuild mapping
vgchange -ay


7. Restore deleted LV (advanced scenario)
Step 1: Stop writes immediately
Step 2: Check archive
ls /etc/lvm/archive

Step 3: Restore older metadata
vgcfgrestore -f /etc/lvm/archive/myvg_xxx.vg myvg

Step 4: Activate
vgchange -ay

👉 Works only if data blocks not overwritten


8. Difference: /etc/lvm/backup vs archive
backup → latest config
archive → historical versions


9. Real production scenario (boot failure due to LVM)
Step 1: Boot rescue mode
Step 2: Activate VG
vgchange -ay

Step 3: Mount root
mount /dev/myvg/root /mnt

Step 4: Fix issue


10. Important commands for recovery
Backup:
vgcfgbackup
Restore:
vgcfgrestore myvg
Scan:
pvscan
vgscan
Activate:
vgchange -ay
Check:
lvs
vgs
pvs


11. Interview tricky question
What happens if /etc/fstab has wrong entry ?
Step 1: Boot may fail or hang
Step 2: System goes into emergency mode
Step 3: Manual fix required

What happens if LVM metadata is lost ?
Step 1: VG not visible
Step 2: Data inaccessible
Step 3: Restore using
vgcfgrestore

